The last six items ask which schools the student went to.
What we are after is the school's type---run by the municipality
(\emph{kommunal huvudman}) or by an independent principal
(\emph{enskild huvudman}, a \emph{friskola})---which may bear on the
approach to learning a student brings to the course
(\cref{prior-knowledge}).
We do not ask the student for the type, though, because it is not a
fact they reliably have: many do not know who ran their school, and a
school that has changed hands since would make even a confident answer
wrong.
The register does know.
So these items collect only what a student can state without judging
anything---the name of the school---and the type is determined
afterwards, when the answers are coded, by looking each school up in
Skolverket's Skolenhetsregistret, which records the huvudman of every
school unit in Sweden (\cref{app:quiz-analysis}).

A name on its own will not resolve there: Swedish school names repeat
across the country, and there is a Vasaskolan in several
municipalities, so each item asks for the municipality on the same line
as the name---that pair is what picks out one school unit.
Students who went to school outside Sweden write the country instead,
which the stems say: the register cannot resolve those at all, and the
country is what tells the coder so, rather than a foreign name that
might be taken for a Swedish one.

The stages are separate items because the huvudman can differ between
them: a student may have gone to a municipal lågstadiet and an
independent högstadiet, and one answer would hide the switch.
The gymnasieprogram is separate for the opposite reason---a programme
is not a school unit and has no huvudman to look up---but it records
what the student studied, which the school name does not.
Every item may be left blank, and every stem says so, because there is
nothing to invent when there is nothing to report: not everyone has
taken a basår, and not everyone went to school in Sweden.

