\subsection{Analysing the results}
\label{app:quiz-analysis}

Canvas compiles the responses into a student-analysis report: a CSV
file with one row per student and one column per item, keyed by the
item titles above.
The analysis program fetches that report itself---we know the course
and the quiz titles, so nothing needs to be clicked or downloaded by
hand---using the same [[canvaslms]] machinery as the command-line tool.
It then tabulates the closed items and writes the open-text answers to
a coding sheet: a CSV file with one row per student and columns for the
codes.
We code all the data we asked for, so that every collected answer is
usable: from the languages answer the languages themselves, roughly how
much they were used, and whether Python is among them; from the
debugging exercise whether the described steps exhibit contrast,
generalisation or fusion thinking (\cref{analysis,mq:agreement}); and
from the school answers the type of each school, which is looked up
rather than judged (\cref{app:background-items}).

The coding is done by a human, but a large language model prepares it:
with [[--llm]] the program asks a model (via the [[llm]] package) to
suggest the codes for each answer, and the human coder verifies or
overrides them in the sheet.
The suggested codes are kept in separate columns from the verified
ones, so the sheet always shows which is which.
The per-item texts of \cref{app:debugging-items} are for the student
and never enter this program: the coding sheet and the pre-coding
prompt carry the students' own answers alone, so what we expect an
answer to show is put in front of neither the coder nor the model.

<<[[analyze_quiz.py]]>>=
