\subsubsection{Who a row belongs to}
\label{app:student-identity}

The item titles join the tangled files to the report; what joins the
report's \emph{rows} to each other is the student, and there a name
will not do.
Two students can share a name, and they need not be in different
courses for that: one course is enough, and this study has only one.
The identifier we use instead is the student's \emph{login ID}---an
email address, unique across the institution, and the identifier the
surrounding [[canvaslms]] machinery already keys results by.
The student-analysis report does not carry it, so [[fetch_report]]
attaches it to every row from the course's own roster
(\cref{app:login-lookup}).

Every join goes through [[student_of]], and through it alone: the
survey program of \cref{app:rspq} pairs a student's two administrations
with it, and it is also what that program writes into the [[student]]
column of [[rspq.csv]], the key the episode analysis
(\cref{app:episodes}) joins on.
One function is what keeps a join from quietly degrading---a join
written against [[STUDENT]] would work on every test data set anyone
would think to make, and fail only on the pair of real students it was
supposed to keep apart.
Rows read from a saved report have no login column, and those fall back
to the course and the name.
Within one course that is the name alone, so a saved report cannot tell
namesakes apart at all; the course still prefixes the fallback, which
makes a fallback key visibly not a login ID and leaves room for a
second course later.
Nothing here can repair a report that lacks the column, but the program
can refuse to guess, which is why the one lookup that has to resolve a
name warns instead of choosing (\cref{app:login-lookup}).

<<the course and the item titles are the keys>>=
LOGIN_COLUMN = "login"


def student_of(row):
    """Return the identifier of the student behind a row.

    The login ID attached by fetch_report when available; rows read
    from a saved report lack it and fall back to course and name.
    """
    return row.get(LOGIN_COLUMN) or f"{COURSE}/{row[STUDENT]}"
@

